00:09:41.240 --> 00:09:46.600
The people that built these facilities are
the industrial experts Spinlaunch had to draw

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:51.870
from, and most thought they would never be
able build a vacuum chamber this large on

00:09:51.870 --> 00:09:53.100
their budget.

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:56.160
But Spinlaunch had some things on their side.

00:09:56.160 --> 00:10:02.080
They didn’t need that extreme of a vacuum,
as their goal is not to simulate the vacuum

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:03.330
of space.

00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:09.630
Their goal is to minimize drag and the power
required to overcome it, minimize the aerodynamic

00:10:09.630 --> 00:10:15.050
heating that would destroy the tether, and
eliminate all those pesky aerodynamics effects

00:10:15.050 --> 00:10:16.620
like flutter.

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:22.250
That means Spinlaunch could use cheaper materials
like mild steel, where ultra high vacuums

00:10:22.250 --> 00:10:26.800
need more expensive specialized processed
materials to avoid outgassing,

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:31.110
where gasses within the metal in the form
of oxides, or simply dissolved within the

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:33.900
metal, are released into the vacuum.

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:38.000
It also makes the process of drawing a vacuum
much easier.

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:42.710
Drawing a vacuum isn’t as simple as just
turning on a pump and leaving it on long enough.

00:10:42.710 --> 00:10:46.140
The more air you draw out, the harder it becomes.

00:10:46.140 --> 00:10:52.140
As you are not only working against a continually
growing pressure gradient, but statistical

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:53.140
probability.

00:10:53.140 --> 00:10:54.140
[5]

00:10:54.140 --> 00:10:59.170
The first stage of drawing a vacuum is to
remove the bulk gas, at this stage the gas

00:10:59.170 --> 00:11:05.390
is a viscous fluid, and the molecules within
the chamber interact with each other often.

00:11:05.390 --> 00:11:10.839
Here we can use traditional fluid flow pumps,
like a positive displacement pump.

00:11:10.839 --> 00:11:15.670
That mechanically moves molecules out of the
chamber, and higher pressure air at the back

00:11:15.670 --> 00:11:19.600
of the chamber forces air to fill the space
created.

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:21.600
Allowing more air to be pumped out.

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:27.340
But, as gas is removed from the chamber the
distance between the molecules increases.

00:11:27.340 --> 00:11:29.840
This is called the mean free path.

00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:34.800
The distance a molecule can travel without
colliding with another molecule.

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:40.790
Now, pressure is really just molecules colliding,
and as collisions become more infrequent,

00:11:40.790 --> 00:11:46.430
the pressure gradients that are needed to
achieve equilibrium begin to vanish.

00:11:46.430 --> 00:11:52.470
Meaning it takes longer and longer for equilibrium
to be established, and the rate the pump can

00:11:52.470 --> 00:11:58.310
remove molecules lowers, as there are simply
fewer and fewer molecules near the pump to

00:11:58.310 --> 00:11:59.510
remove.

00:11:59.510 --> 00:12:05.770
At some point viscous flow stops entirely
and we enter a flow regime called molecular

00:12:05.770 --> 00:12:06.770
flow.

00:12:06.770 --> 00:12:12.520
Where the distance between collisions is actually
larger than the internal dimensions of the

00:12:12.520 --> 00:12:13.800
vacuum chamber.

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:19.620
Meaning, the molecules are statistically more
likely to just bounce inside the chamber with

00:12:19.620 --> 00:12:22.960
nothing forcing them towards the exit.

00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:28.050
At this stage it is impossible to actively
pump the molecules out.

00:12:28.050 --> 00:12:34.250
The molecular pumps needed for this flow regime
instead act like some kind of Venus fly trap,

00:12:34.250 --> 00:12:39.620
waiting for a molecule to enter it, and then
its job is to prevent the molecule from returning

00:12:39.620 --> 00:12:41.890
to the original chamber.

00:12:41.890 --> 00:12:46.680
Turbomolecular pumps are basically multiple
levels of turbines that knock molecules in

00:12:46.680 --> 00:12:50.400
one direction and prevent them from traveling
backwards.

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:58.170
These pumps require insane rotation speeds,
anywhere from 36,000 rpm to 72,000 rpm and

00:12:58.170 --> 00:13:04.590
need incredibly tight tolerances too; They
are trying to pump individual molecules after

00:13:04.590 --> 00:13:05.590
all.

00:13:05.590 --> 00:13:10.160
So, it goes without saying, these kinds of
pumps are expensive.

00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:15.720
All the while, outgassing and other leaks
are actively working against the game of pure

00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:17.029
chance.

00:13:17.029 --> 00:13:23.220
Creating a high vacuum requires extreme precision
in manufacturing and design.

