00:26:10.470 --> 00:26:15.500
Spinlaunch claims their rockets will need
to carry about 30% of the fuel and oxidiser

00:26:15.500 --> 00:26:17.550
compared to these competitors, with substantially
miniaturized and simplified rocket components.

00:26:17.550 --> 00:26:23.150
They are essentially replacing the first stage
of a traditional rocket with an easily reusable

00:26:23.150 --> 00:26:25.630
kinetic launch system.

00:26:25.630 --> 00:26:30.580
Spinlaunch will also be able to recapture
a good deal of the electricity stored as kinetic

00:26:30.580 --> 00:26:34.410
energy in the tether, using regenerative braking.

00:26:34.410 --> 00:26:37.630
Even further reducing their electricity bill.

00:26:37.630 --> 00:26:42.100
Because of all this, the limiting factor for
spinlaunch in terms of weight is actually

00:26:42.100 --> 00:26:47.090
the weight the tether can support, and as
a result, it actually makes sense to maximize

00:26:47.090 --> 00:26:53.160
the density of the aeroshell, because it affects
a variable that will drastically improve its

00:26:53.160 --> 00:26:55.950
ability to punch through the atmosphere.

00:26:55.950 --> 00:26:58.700
It’s ballistic coefficient.

00:26:58.700 --> 00:27:03.429
Ballistic coefficient is essentially an object's
ability to resist air resistance.

00:27:03.429 --> 00:27:05.940
Think about how hard it is to throw a feather.

00:27:05.940 --> 00:27:09.740
No matter how hard you throw it, it’s not
going to go very far.

00:27:09.740 --> 00:27:15.230
It’s got a large surface area for air resistance
to act upon relative to its weight.

00:27:15.230 --> 00:27:18.750
That’s a low ballistic coefficient.

00:27:18.750 --> 00:27:23.940
Ballistic coefficient is found by dividing
the mass of the projective by the drag coefficient

00:27:23.940 --> 00:27:27.000
multiplied by the cross-sectional area.

00:27:27.000 --> 00:27:33.450
So spinlaunch effectively wants to maximize
the mass relative to the cross sectional area.

00:27:33.450 --> 00:27:37.630
This is obviously not typical for aerospace
vehicles.

00:27:37.630 --> 00:27:44.460
If you, if you look at reentry capsules whether
it's for something like the Stardust return

00:27:44.460 --> 00:27:48.460
capsule where it's really, really high velocity
or you look at it reentry from orbit for a

00:27:48.460 --> 00:27:51.090
manned capsule or something like the space
shuttle.

00:27:51.090 --> 00:27:55.800
They're typically using thermal protection
systems that are extremely low density, like

00:27:55.800 --> 00:27:59.370
on the order of less than 300 kilograms per
cubic meter.

00:27:59.370 --> 00:28:01.370
It's just basically foam.

00:28:01.370 --> 00:28:07.030
And so so typically that means you're making
like significant compromises, like.

00:28:07.030 --> 00:28:08.400
The material often is.

00:28:08.400 --> 00:28:14.450
You know, brittle Or prone to fracture you
know, or really expensive or gets worn away.

00:28:14.450 --> 00:28:19.400
And then you have to replace the tiles, kind
of in the infamous case of the space shuttle.

00:28:19.400 --> 00:28:22.270
So what we're dealing with is, you know, you're
on the tip of the vehicle.

00:28:22.270 --> 00:28:27.370
You have, you know, materials like copper,
which, you know, not only are they, you know,

00:28:27.370 --> 00:28:29.400
a significantly higher density, right?

00:28:29.400 --> 00:28:34.659
You're talking about, you know, thousands
of kilograms per cubic meter, but they also

00:28:34.659 --> 00:28:36.950
have really great thermal conductivity.

00:28:36.950 --> 00:28:42.600
So basically, as you transition through the
atmosphere, you have a high heat load, but

00:28:42.600 --> 00:28:47.580
then you're basically into heavy, dense materials
that have good thermal conductivity.

00:28:47.580 --> 00:28:51.919
This is one of those unintuitive consequences
of this style of launch.

00:28:51.919 --> 00:28:56.460
When I first saw the full-scale aeroshell
on the Spinlaunch factory floor, I first asked

00:28:56.460 --> 00:29:01.430
if I could ride it like a cowboy, but then
immediately noticed the bi-metallic nose cone.

00:29:01.430 --> 00:29:05.320
I knew from looking at it that it was made
from copper and aluminum, and that struck

00:29:05.320 --> 00:29:07.730
me as extremely odd.

00:29:07.730 --> 00:29:12.560
Those metals would melt at the temperatures
I associate with hypersonic speeds.

00:29:12.560 --> 00:29:17.750
But, because spinlaunch launches at Mach 6,
it actually transitions through the lower

00:29:17.750 --> 00:29:22.670
atmosphere rather quickly, and as a result,
the heat generated can simply be absorbed

00:29:22.670 --> 00:29:24.860
by these large heat sinks.

00:29:24.860 --> 00:29:30.380
Aluminum and copper's high thermal conductivity
means the heat is distributed through the

00:29:30.380 --> 00:29:35.400
body of the aeroshell before it has a chance
to damage the vehicle.

00:29:35.400 --> 00:29:39.789
The hefty carbon fiber shell is also incredibly
strong.

00:29:39.789 --> 00:29:44.210
Spinlaunch has already pulled their smaller
scale aeroshells out of the ground, buried

00:29:44.210 --> 00:29:50.650
several feet deep from the force of impact,
and reused them with minimal refurbishment.

00:29:50.650 --> 00:29:56.980
With a parachute these aeroshells will be
fully reusable with minimal maintenance, especially

00:29:56.980 --> 00:30:02.200
as they serve no function other than to protect
the inner rocket’s stages.

00:30:02.200 --> 00:30:05.919
This isn’t an intricate mechanical machine.

00:30:05.919 --> 00:30:09.360
Launching from the ground at these speeds
comes with advantages too.

00:30:09.360 --> 00:30:15.320
If we plot drag coefficient vs mach number
for a bullet like projectile something rather

00:30:15.320 --> 00:30:16.630
unintuitive occurs.

00:30:16.630 --> 00:30:22.520
The drag coefficient rises as you would expect
up until we hit Mach 1, at this point it starts

00:30:22.520 --> 00:30:25.539
to fall as Mach number increases.

00:30:25.539 --> 00:30:27.440
This is the equation for drag.

00:30:27.440 --> 00:30:31.620
It’s proportional to drag coefficient, air
density and velocity squared.

00:30:31.620 --> 00:30:36.440
With drag coefficient being lower at hypersonic
speeds, it actually makes some sense to punch

00:30:36.440 --> 00:30:41.280
through the thick lower atmosphere, where
the high density air causes drag to rise,

00:30:41.280 --> 00:30:43.660
as fast as possible.

00:30:43.660 --> 00:30:49.200
Deceleration is a function of time after all,
meters per second square, meters per second

00:30:49.200 --> 00:30:50.830
lost per second.

00:30:50.830 --> 00:30:56.809
Let’s calculate the dynamic pressure this
drag would create at launch, and the deceleration

00:30:56.809 --> 00:30:58.290
it would cause.

00:30:58.290 --> 00:31:03.809
The dynamic pressure is found by multiplying
air density by the velocity squared and dividing

00:31:03.809 --> 00:31:04.929
by 2.

00:31:04.929 --> 00:31:10.669
At sea level, at Mach 6, the dynamic pressure
will be 2.6 Megapascals.

00:31:10.669 --> 00:31:16.270
The final aeroshell is 1 meter in diameter
and has a drag coefficient of about 0.1.

00:31:16.270 --> 00:31:22.539
Which means the force applied to the aeroshell
at launch will be 205 kNs.

00:31:22.539 --> 00:31:26.900
This sounds like a lot, but here's where the
ballistic coefficient comes in.

00:31:26.900 --> 00:31:32.330
This drag force is being applied to a 10 tonne
body moving at mach 6.

00:31:32.330 --> 00:31:35.200
That’s a lot of inertia.

00:31:35.200 --> 00:31:38.279
Force equals mass by acceleration.

00:31:38.279 --> 00:31:41.500
Acceleration equals force divided by mass.

00:31:41.500 --> 00:31:45.340
That means high mass equals less deceleration.

00:31:45.340 --> 00:31:52.940
In this case deceleration due to drag will
be about 19.8 m/s per second at launch, but

00:31:52.940 --> 00:31:58.870
it will rapidly decrease as we move through
to thinner and thinner layers of the atmosphere

00:31:58.870 --> 00:31:59.950
and lose velocity.

00:31:59.950 --> 00:32:06.169
In fact, with Spinlaunch’s planned trajectory,
we can plot the atmospheric density the aeroshell

00:32:06.169 --> 00:32:08.390
will encounter over time.

00:32:08.390 --> 00:32:13.980
Halfing in just 5 seconds, and dropping to
less that 10% of the original air density

00:32:13.980 --> 00:32:16.070
in 15 seconds.

00:32:16.070 --> 00:32:22.000
While gravity will remain more or less constant
at 9.8 m/s per second.

00:32:22.000 --> 00:32:28.159
That means gravity loses form the majority
of energy losses in our transition to orbit.

00:32:28.159 --> 00:32:38.160
In total spinlaunch will lose about 150 m/s
of velocity to drag and 1000 m/s to gravity.

00:32:38.160 --> 00:32:46.100
Satellites like Starlink orbit at 500 kilometers
with a velocity of about 7700 m/s, so even

00:32:46.100 --> 00:32:53.470
if Spinlaunch maintained it 2050 m/s velocity
from launch up until the aeroshell broke apart,

00:32:53.470 --> 00:32:58.460
the 2 stage rocket hidden within would still
have its work cut out for it.

00:32:58.460 --> 00:33:03.850
However, now free of the mass of the aeroshell,
the substantially miniaturized rocket needs

00:33:03.850 --> 00:33:10.610
only a fraction of the mass of fuel and oxidiser
to rapidly accelerate the 200 kilogram satellite,

00:33:10.610 --> 00:33:15.780
the largest satellite this system can launch,
through the thin atmosphere at this altitude.

00:33:15.780 --> 00:33:19.770
We can actually graph the relative velocity
of the spacecraft over time.

00:33:19.770 --> 00:33:27.220
It Started at Mach 6 at launch, and ended
up at about 1500 m/s when the aeroshell splits.

00:33:27.220 --> 00:33:34.360
The rocket motors then kick in to rapidly
accelerate the satellite to its 7700 m/s orbital

00:33:34.360 --> 00:33:35.730
velocity.

00:33:35.730 --> 00:33:40.260
The physics here absolutely checks out here,
but whether the economics and cost of development

00:33:40.260 --> 00:33:44.590
will be viable is the big question to be answered.

00:33:44.590 --> 00:33:49.480
Spinlaunch has built a 1 third scale prototype
at a relatively low cost, but the hardest

00:33:49.480 --> 00:33:52.159
part of this technology is scale.

00:33:52.159 --> 00:33:58.000
They have reached 1.6 mach thus far, have
tested their satellite components at 10,000g

00:33:58.000 --> 00:34:02.669
in their test facility in Long Beach, and
are continually upping their test parameters,

00:34:02.669 --> 00:34:05.370
pushing further and further.

00:34:05.370 --> 00:34:11.010
This is a comparison of SpaceX and Spinlaunch’s
proposed launch trajectory, but it doesn’t

00:34:11.010 --> 00:34:14.869
tell the full story of the real driving issue
here, economics.

00:34:14.869 --> 00:34:20.389
A SpaceX launch to low earth orbit costs about
67 millions dollars.

00:34:20.389 --> 00:34:27.840
The heaviest Falcon 9 payload to date has
been 16,250 kg on a densely packed starlink

00:34:27.840 --> 00:34:28.840
mission.

00:34:28.840 --> 00:34:33.580
[14] That equates to a launch price of about
4100 dollars per kilogram.

00:34:33.580 --> 00:34:38.220
However, small satellite launch companies,
like Rocket Lab, who offer greater control

00:34:38.220 --> 00:34:46.099
over orbit and launch schedules, charge about
15,000 to 25,000 dollars per kilogram.

00:34:46.099 --> 00:34:50.609
Dollars per kilogram is not a perfect metric,
but gives us some idea of the competition

00:34:50.609 --> 00:34:53.330
Spinlaunch is facing.

00:34:53.330 --> 00:34:57.410
Spinlaunch’s main competitive advantage
is in the decrease of expendable materials

00:34:57.410 --> 00:35:01.210
like fuel while substantially miniaturized
rocket components.

00:35:01.210 --> 00:35:06.640
They also have huge potential to launch far
more frequently than their competitors, helping

00:35:06.640 --> 00:35:09.700
the economics of scale to kick in.

00:35:09.700 --> 00:35:14.030
Spinlaunch claims to be targeting an ambitious
per launch price in the range of half a million

00:35:14.030 --> 00:35:19.020
dollars, placing them at 2500 dollars per
kilogram.

00:35:19.020 --> 00:35:23.450
In my time in Spinlaunch, talking to their
engineers, it’s clear they are excited and

00:35:23.450 --> 00:35:25.359
believe in this company.

00:35:25.359 --> 00:35:30.210
The basic napkin physics for Spinlaunch absolutely
checks out, and they are well on their way

00:35:30.210 --> 00:35:35.570
of solving the engineering challenges, but
scaling up this monstrous engineering effort

00:35:35.570 --> 00:35:41.619
is going to require enormous amounts of investment,
and Spinlaunch could not disclose the answer

00:35:41.619 --> 00:35:45.720
to many of my questions, as they seek patents
for their solutions.

00:35:45.720 --> 00:35:50.930
I got little info on one of the most difficult
parts of the launch system, the release mechanism

00:35:50.930 --> 00:35:56.960
for the aeroshell; even the 3D models Spinlaunch
provided for this video had the release mechanism

00:35:56.960 --> 00:36:02.050
removed, so we had to model our own along
with the internal rocket structure.

00:36:02.050 --> 00:36:06.710
The design of the satellites is another problem,
due to the massive gs the satellites have

00:36:06.710 --> 00:36:12.270
to survive, but g-hardening isn’t as large
an engineering challenge as the internet seems

00:36:12.270 --> 00:36:13.470
to think.

00:36:13.470 --> 00:36:14.740
The most difficult part.

00:36:14.740 --> 00:36:17.550
So besides the structure is, is also the reaction
wheel.

00:36:17.550 --> 00:36:21.820
So the reaction wheel is, generates momentum
and basically steals the bus.

00:36:21.820 --> 00:36:22.820
Yeah.

00:36:22.820 --> 00:36:26.980
And so it typically is a big mass that's cantilevered
up at a certain angle.

00:36:26.980 --> 00:36:30.650
So which is the one thing that we don't like,
you don't want having a big mass sticking

00:36:30.650 --> 00:36:31.650
up on a can really.

00:36:31.650 --> 00:36:34.660
This is what I assumed was going to be like
a difficult thing to because it inherently

00:36:34.660 --> 00:36:38.109
has to be fairly high mass to control the
satellite.

00:36:38.109 --> 00:36:39.109
Right.

00:36:39.109 --> 00:36:42.780
And so we've done a lot of work to instead
of re-engineering the wheel itself and figure

00:36:42.780 --> 00:36:46.080
out different ways to do that, we basically
just took and created it took a clever way

00:36:46.080 --> 00:36:47.270
of deploying the wheel.

00:36:47.270 --> 00:36:51.290
So we support the wheel in the flat orientation
and we spin.

00:36:51.290 --> 00:36:53.310
So when it's spinning, it's, it's well supported.

00:36:53.310 --> 00:36:56.560
The bearings are unloaded and so it can spin
and do its thing.

00:36:56.560 --> 00:37:00.690
And then we deploy the wheel for when it actually
used to operate.

00:37:00.690 --> 00:37:01.690
Okay.

00:37:01.690 --> 00:37:05.960
So it's a it's a simple solution for what
could have been a really difficult problem.

00:37:05.960 --> 00:37:10.650
And does the the axis of the actual wheel
cause any issues when it's like being loaded?

00:37:10.650 --> 00:37:14.550
I imagine that's a fairly high weight to have
on the axle yeah.

00:37:14.550 --> 00:37:18.840
So we, what we do is we unload the bearings
and as part of the deployment mechanism we

00:37:18.840 --> 00:37:21.360
actually move we reload the wheel into the
bearings.

00:37:21.360 --> 00:37:22.360
Oh, okay.

00:37:22.360 --> 00:37:23.360
So it's just taken off completely Okay.

00:37:23.360 --> 00:37:24.360
That's interesting.

00:37:24.360 --> 00:37:25.360
Okay, that's funny.

00:37:25.360 --> 00:37:26.360
Yeah.

00:37:26.360 --> 00:37:27.770
So again, trying to make simple solutions
for very difficult problems.

00:37:27.770 --> 00:37:28.770
Yeah.

00:37:28.770 --> 00:37:29.770
I like that.

00:37:29.770 --> 00:37:32.210
And like those are very simple answers, right?

00:37:32.210 --> 00:37:36.000
Like I figured that the like the inertial
wheels would be difficult, not like you just

00:37:36.000 --> 00:37:40.050
think about it as like, yeah, that's actually
a fairly easy thing to just not deal with.

00:37:40.050 --> 00:37:43.300
You don't have to have it in the exact configuration
when you like launch, right?

00:37:43.300 --> 00:37:47.869
Same with the solar panels.You can have them,
like you said, loaded.

00:37:47.869 --> 00:37:48.869
Yeah.

00:37:48.869 --> 00:37:52.820
So it's the problems aren't necessarily hard
to deal with.

00:37:52.820 --> 00:37:54.510
It's just you have to think differently.

00:37:54.510 --> 00:37:55.510
Right?

00:37:55.510 --> 00:37:56.510
And so, you know, Randy, who you talk to earlier,
that's one of the things he's been saying

00:37:56.510 --> 00:37:57.510
a lot is it's not that these are difficult
problems.

00:37:57.510 --> 00:37:58.940
We just have to change the way we think about
design.

00:37:58.940 --> 00:38:00.930
So it's a little bit it's not a lot.

00:38:00.930 --> 00:38:06.260
The nice part is to that over the last 60
years, what people have been trying to do

00:38:06.260 --> 00:38:10.060
with satellites actually has helped us because
they want to reduce mass.

00:38:10.060 --> 00:38:11.470
They want to make things stronger.

00:38:11.470 --> 00:38:17.180
So every bit that they're forcing them to
deal with shock and vibe already helps us.

00:38:17.180 --> 00:38:19.520
And it already inherently starts to make them
more hardened.

00:38:19.520 --> 00:38:21.760
Most components, we don't have to do anything
to them.

00:38:21.760 --> 00:38:26.190
Maybe a little epoxy here but like we one
of the most surprising events that we have

00:38:26.190 --> 00:38:30.050
here among the entire team is we took a board
that had a password stuck up, you know, maybe

00:38:30.050 --> 00:38:31.160
a quarter of an inch.

00:38:31.160 --> 00:38:34.060
And we all looked I was like, okay, that thing's
it's going to fly off the wall.

00:38:34.060 --> 00:38:37.770
And we spun it and we brought it back and
